From 74cbc4046d516c7481ebb4400bc117d082b54baf Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: David Reed <reedswork@proton.me>
Date: Tue, 17 Nov 2026 18:00:00 -0500
Subject: [PATCH] client: TLS sessions are kept as Chrome keeps them, by host
 and port, 1024 in all

rustls keyed its session cache by server name alone. So a connection to
another port of a host offered that port's TLS 1.3 PSK or TLS 1.2 ticket,
and a TLS 1.3 ticket from one port took the place of the TLS 1.2 session
another port's server would have resumed. Chrome's SSLClientSessionCache
is keyed by host and port, so its hello to a port it has not met carries
an empty ticket and no PSK.

Each provider (each network partition) now keeps one cache, shared by its
HTTP, WebSocket and raw TLS connections, of Chrome's size:
- at most 1024 host and port keys, the least recently used dropped first;
- at most two TLS 1.3 tickets and one TLS 1.2 session per key.
Each connection gets a view of it for its own port. Nothing is held for a
port until it gives a session or a key share hint, so a page that reaches
thousands of ports leaves at most 1024 entries behind.
